1
00:00:00,800 --> 00:00:02,499
When people work side by side,

2
00:00:02,499 --> 00:00:05,613
a glance or a gesture is often enough to be understood.

3
00:00:06,463 --> 00:00:10,707
Can an AI assistant that sees through our eyes collaborate the same way?

4
00:00:16,057 --> 00:00:19,840
Imagine organizing a crowded family bookshelf, with Dad's classics,

5
00:00:19,840 --> 00:00:22,776
your sister's picture books, and your own textbooks,

6
00:00:22,776 --> 00:00:25,148
helped by an AI assistant in your glasses.

7
00:00:25,998 --> 00:00:30,149
Today's assistants make you translate what you see and do into words.

8
00:00:30,149 --> 00:00:34,600
Rich, parallel physical intent gets squeezed into a single verbal channel.

9
00:00:34,600 --> 00:00:36,464
This is the communication gulf.

10
00:00:37,314 --> 00:00:41,065
And even when the AI recognizes objects, it misses embodied cues,

11
00:00:41,065 --> 00:00:44,065
like a hesitant gaze or a pause with a book in hand,

12
00:00:44,065 --> 00:00:47,122
and it forgets the personal rules you just agreed on.

13
00:00:47,122 --> 00:00:48,911
This is the understanding gulf.

14
00:00:49,761 --> 00:00:50,397
So we ask:

15
00:00:50,397 --> 00:00:53,447
how can a shared first-person perspective enable

16
00:00:53,447 --> 00:00:56,816
continuous cognitive alignment between humans and AI?

17
00:00:57,666 --> 00:00:58,911
We introduce Eye2Eye,

18
00:00:58,911 --> 00:01:01,935
a framework that turns the first-person view from a

19
00:01:01,935 --> 00:01:05,137
passive input into a shared channel for collaboration.

20
00:01:05,137 --> 00:01:07,331
It closes a loop of three components.

21
00:01:08,181 --> 00:01:08,428
One:

22
00:01:08,428 --> 00:01:09,415
joint attention.

23
00:01:09,415 --> 00:01:14,286
From human to AI, Eye2Eye captures speech, gaze, and gestures such as pointing,

24
00:01:14,286 --> 00:01:18,602
touching, and grasping, to detect interaction events and infer intent.

25
00:01:19,452 --> 00:01:23,785
From AI to human, it makes its own focus visible through visual highlights,

26
00:01:23,785 --> 00:01:27,771
voice, and text labels, so both partners converge on the same object.

27
00:01:28,621 --> 00:01:28,864
Two:

28
00:01:28,864 --> 00:01:30,442
accumulated common ground.

29
00:01:30,442 --> 00:01:33,597
Each interaction is stored in a dynamic object card:

30
00:01:33,597 --> 00:01:36,207
what the object is, what the user intended,

31
00:01:36,207 --> 00:01:38,877
how the AI responded, and whether it worked.

32
00:01:38,877 --> 00:01:42,821
Cards persist, and they are revised whenever the AI is corrected.

33
00:01:43,671 --> 00:01:44,021
Three:

34
00:01:44,021 --> 00:01:45,714
reflective situated feedback.

35
00:01:45,714 --> 00:01:49,334
Using that common ground, the AI picks one of four strategies:

36
00:01:49,334 --> 00:01:52,428
object recognition, error checking, knowledge recall,

37
00:01:52,428 --> 00:01:55,638
or action planning, each mixing visual and verbal cues.

38
00:01:55,638 --> 00:02:00,017
It then watches how the user reacts, and writes the outcome back to memory.

39
00:02:00,867 --> 00:02:05,404
Together, these components form a continuous perceive, align, and reflect loop.

40
00:02:06,254 --> 00:02:08,677
Our prototype runs on Apple Vision Pro.

41
00:02:08,677 --> 00:02:11,473
YOLO tracks objects in the first-person view.

42
00:02:11,473 --> 00:02:15,760
When a hand overlaps an object, or gaze dwells on it for six seconds,

43
00:02:15,760 --> 00:02:20,731
an event fires, and GPT-4o interprets the frame together with the user's speech.

44
00:02:21,581 --> 00:02:23,967
That state is embedded and matched against

45
00:02:23,967 --> 00:02:26,411
existing object cards by cosine similarity.

46
00:02:26,411 --> 00:02:29,706
The best match is retrieved, fused with the current state,

47
00:02:29,706 --> 00:02:31,695
and the card is created or updated.

48
00:02:32,545 --> 00:02:36,159
Finally, the model selects a feedback strategy and modality,

49
00:02:36,159 --> 00:02:39,352
rendered in AR and spoken with Gemini text-to-speech,

50
00:02:39,352 --> 00:02:41,822
in about four to five seconds end to end.

51
00:02:42,672 --> 00:02:44,860
Let's walk through a complete example.

52
00:02:46,510 --> 00:02:48,013
USER: What kind of book is this?

53
00:02:48,363 --> 00:02:50,307
AI: "Great by Choice" is a business book.

54
00:02:51,957 --> 00:02:53,268
When the user hesitates,

55
00:02:53,268 --> 00:02:57,310
Eye2Eye picks up the cue from gaze and hand movement, without being asked.

56
00:02:57,660 --> 00:03:00,578
AI: I recommend the Toolkit category for easy access,

57
00:03:00,578 --> 00:03:02,484
AI: rather than the Design category.

58
00:03:04,134 --> 00:03:05,523
USER: I should put this book in...

59
00:03:05,873 --> 00:03:08,369
AI: You can put this book in the Biology section.

60
00:03:08,719 --> 00:03:11,091
USER: I don't think Biology is the right fit.

61
00:03:11,091 --> 00:03:15,956
USER: My little sister loves these books specifically, not the whole Biology category.

62
00:03:17,606 --> 00:03:21,487
AI: Okay, let's create a new section for this, called Children's Reading.

63
00:03:21,837 --> 00:03:24,682
The correction is written back into the object card.

64
00:03:26,332 --> 00:03:27,800
USER: Where should I put this one?

65
00:03:28,150 --> 00:03:31,136
AI: The dinosaur book should go in Children's Reading as well.

66
00:03:32,786 --> 00:03:34,892
USER: These books aren't on the same theme.

67
00:03:34,892 --> 00:03:36,714
USER: Do they go in the same category?

68
00:03:37,064 --> 00:03:38,693
AI: Based on our earlier agreement,

69
00:03:38,693 --> 00:03:42,740
AI: these books are mainly images and photographs, so they belong in Visual Arts.

70
00:03:44,390 --> 00:03:46,577
USER: Let's put this in Expression Training.

71
00:03:46,927 --> 00:03:48,653
AI: That category isn't quite right.

72
00:03:48,653 --> 00:03:52,591
AI: Thinking Training keeps it with the rest of the Pyramid Principle series.

73
00:03:54,241 --> 00:03:59,193
Beyond bookshelves, the same loop guides procedural tasks like operating a coffee machine,

74
00:03:59,193 --> 00:04:02,385
and inspection tasks like troubleshooting a circuit board.

75
00:04:03,235 --> 00:04:07,408
We compared Eye2Eye with a baseline using the same headset and models,

76
00:04:07,408 --> 00:04:10,449
in a study with sixty participants and three tasks.

77
00:04:11,299 --> 00:04:17,059
Eye2Eye reduced the likelihood of errors by about fifty-eight percent, cut clarification turns by half,

78
00:04:17,059 --> 00:04:21,365
and reduced interaction turns in the procedural task by twenty-three percent.

79
00:04:22,215 --> 00:04:25,072
Errors also accumulated far more slowly over time,

80
00:04:25,072 --> 00:04:29,073
and participants rated the collaboration as significantly more fluent,

81
00:04:29,073 --> 00:04:31,245
more co-present, and more trustworthy.

82
00:04:32,095 --> 00:04:36,506
By sharing what we see, humans and AI can do more than look at the same thing.

83
00:04:36,506 --> 00:04:38,202
They can truly see eye to eye.

